A||N||10||Songman||5 speed help||||1||
Z||000000||Songman||10-02-2000||03:31 PM||||What a weekend I had! Bought an 88 Formula for $500! The guy that had it got it from another guy and he knows absolutely nothing about Fieros.<P>I spent the day working on it and got the little V6 purring like a kitten. Other than a few exhaust leaks I think I am good there.<P>The problem came with the shifter. It would hardly move. I could get the tranny in reverse. I took the console apart and sprayed lubricant all over the shifter workings. I got it moving around freely but still won't pop into gear. I can't find any adjustments.<P>It could be that the tranny is bad. I don't think so because from time to time I can pull it back like going into gear and it will start to grind when I let the clutch out, but not always...<P>Any ideas or anything I should check? Thanks...||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||10-02-2000||03:44 PM||||Sounds like the shift cables need adjusting.  The adjustment is at the trans., where the cables attach at slotted holes in the levers.<P>Another thing that might be of interest:<BR>   <A HREF="http://www.fiero.mn.org/Knowledge/Procedure.asp?Version=o7q4ha73&Catid=21&Id=27&Action=Edit" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.mn.org/Knowledge/Procedure.asp?Version=o7q4ha73&Catid=21&Id=27&Action=Edit</A> <P>Hope this helps  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !||||reg||1
Z||000002||Songman||10-02-2000||05:40 PM||||Okay, I looked back there but couldn't tell anyway to adjust it. Is there some foolproof way to know it is the linkage and not the tranny tore out? What is the best way to go about adjusting the linkage?<P>Maybe I should just tow the thing across the mountain to Ed and let him fix it! hehehe||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||10-02-2000||05:57 PM||||You could tow the thing up to my place and I'll give ya $650 for it! J/K. It really sounds like you have one or two bad cables. If you unbolt the cables from the trans linkage, you should be able to shift the trans fairly easily by moving the levers, you can sorta hear it "clack" into gear. Find any gear, fire it up and try to drive it in that gear. Go through all the gears one at a time to check them out. <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Songman||10-02-2000||06:17 PM||||Good idea, Jon! I'll give that a try. Unfortunately, it is at my place down in GA and I won't get to work on it for a couple of weeks. I was hoping to drive it home to Nashville yesterday but just couldn't get it going in time...<P>Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000005||PJ||10-02-2000||09:34 PM||||There is not much to adjust as far as the cables go. To narrow the problem down, disconnect both cables at the transaxle. There is a shaft running into the transaxle with a short lever at the end. That connects to the shift cable and rotates the shaft left or right (the middle position is neutral). The cable going to the lever going to the pinned collar below the top lever is called the select cable. It moves the shaft up and down. Combine these articulations and you get the six gears available. Grab the top lever and move the shaft left and right with the shaft pulled out, left and right shaft in the middle, left and right shaft pushed in. It should be easy to shift it in this manner into all five foward gears (reverse can be a *****  to engage and I forget which position it is) if the transaxle is in good shape. I've had to replace one or the other cable five times over 12 years. The select cable being routed around the exhaust doesn't help things. It fact there was a service bulletin addressing this problem. The  seals on the cable ends also deteriorate and let moisture in making them stick.<P>Hope this helps ya,<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black||||reg||1
Z||000006||Songman||10-03-2000||10:16 PM||||Of course, I haven't been able to check out my car yet since it is in GA but I was looking in my Fiero Store catalog today and those cable are $250 each!<P>If that is the case, are there cheaper places to get these cables? If it is the tranny, how much am I looking at and how hard is it to replace?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cooter||10-03-2000||11:24 PM||||I bought my new shifter cables at the dealership- I cant remember the exact price, but it wasnt too bad.||||reg||1
Z||000008||PJ||10-04-2000||12:33 AM||||The cables are less than $100 ea. retail at the dealer. I think I pay about $65 with 25% off.<BR>You can find the Getrags at any U Pull It yard although not necessarily in the correct case. They are easy to rebuild and swap internal parts between cases. The hardest part is getting the transaxle out and back in the car.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Songman||10-04-2000||12:44 AM||||Thanks! Let's hope it don't come to that!||||reg||7
